1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,000
{\an8}THE SIGNAL BOX

2
00:00:03,541 --> 00:00:04,633
[rain on the roof]

3
00:00:07,870 --> 00:00:09,991
<i>A lit box above two wet rails.</i>

4
00:00:22,396 --> 00:00:24,495
<i>A row of levers, red, black, blue.</i>

5
00:00:28,556 --> 00:00:30,486
<i>Hello? Am I right for Kilcarn?</i>

6
00:00:31,119 --> 00:00:32,119
Door's open.

7
00:00:32,939 --> 00:00:35,041
[boots on the wooden stairs]

8
00:00:35,531 --> 00:00:38,681
- Dessie Hanlon. Relief.
- You're nine minutes late.

9
00:00:39,230 --> 00:00:42,389
- The bus was held outside the church.
- Coat on the hook.

10
00:00:42,942 --> 00:00:44,843
Is it always this loud up here?

11
00:00:45,302 --> 00:00:48,964
It's the roof. After a year you stop hearing it.

12
00:00:49,647 --> 00:00:51,849
- Agnes, is it? Agnes Toole?
- Agnes.

13
00:00:52,696 --> 00:00:56,402
- How long have you been at it?
- Thirty-eight years.

14
00:00:56,905 --> 00:00:58,726
- In this box?
- In this box.

15
00:00:59,728 --> 00:01:02,484
- That's longer than I'm alive.
- By fourteen years.

16
00:01:03,195 --> 00:01:05,780
- Under the lever frame. Not on it.
- Sorry.

17
00:01:08,481 --> 00:01:12,410
Put the kettle on.
It gives your hands something to do.

18
00:01:22,793 --> 00:01:27,405
- Right. Kettle. Where's the -
- Behind you. Water's in the can.

19
00:01:27,911 --> 00:01:31,423
- There's only the one cup.
- There's two. One's behind the tin.

20
00:01:41,147 --> 00:01:44,169
- What do I do first?
- Nothing. You watch the block.

21
00:01:45,022 --> 00:01:49,521
- I did eight weeks at Inchbawn.
- Inchbawn is a platform and a hut.

22
00:01:50,463 --> 00:01:54,955
Kilcarn Junction. The up line,
the down line, and the branch.

23
00:01:55,674 --> 00:01:57,453
And tonight's the last of it.

24
00:01:58,378 --> 00:01:59,702
Tonight's a Tuesday.

25
00:02:00,234 --> 00:02:04,408
- They told me it was a big night.
- They tell everybody something.

26
00:02:05,316 --> 00:02:09,681
- My mother came out on this branch.
- So did half the county.

27
00:02:19,908 --> 00:02:21,716
[the telephone bell, two rings]

28
00:02:22,565 --> 00:02:23,565
Kilcarn.

29
00:02:24,157 --> 00:02:26,123
<i>Miss Toole. Bracewell here.</i>

30
00:02:26,596 --> 00:02:27,596
Mr. Bracewell.

31
00:02:28,047 --> 00:02:30,376
<i>I take it the relief man arrived.</i>

32
00:02:30,821 --> 00:02:32,405
He's here. Making tea.

33
00:02:33,217 --> 00:02:36,222
<i>Good. Now, as regards tomorrow,
nothing has been settled.</i>

34
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:38,731
Nothing was settled last week.

35
00:02:43,026 --> 00:02:45,250
<i>These things take their course.</i>

36
00:02:45,823 --> 00:02:47,720
The 22:40 is running late.

37
00:02:48,622 --> 00:02:50,534
<i>I'm aware the 22:40 is running.</i>

38
00:02:51,146 --> 00:02:53,052
Then you know more than I do.

39
00:02:53,948 --> 00:02:56,017
<i>I'll telephone again before midnight.</i>

40
00:02:56,888 --> 00:02:58,446
<i>The line clicks and is gone.</i>

41
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:11,485
- Was that him? The inspector?
- That was a man on a telephone.

42
00:03:11,889 --> 00:03:16,271
- He never said what happens to you.
- He never says anything at all.

43
00:03:17,239 --> 00:03:19,303
<i>Dessie runs a hand down the frame.</i>

44
00:03:19,785 --> 00:03:22,969
- How many of these are live?
- Count them.

45
00:03:23,509 --> 00:03:26,132
- Twelve, thirteen -
- Out loud is slower.

46
00:03:26,872 --> 00:03:30,110
Twenty-six, twenty-seven, twenty-eight,
twenty-nine, thirty,

47
00:03:33,785 --> 00:03:34,785
Thirty-one.

48
00:03:42,006 --> 00:03:43,080
Is that right?

49
00:03:43,736 --> 00:03:44,736
No.

50
00:03:45,896 --> 00:03:48,340
- Then which one did I miss?
- You'll find it.

51
00:03:48,977 --> 00:03:52,038
- Rain's going off.
- That's not good news.

52
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:55,653
- Why not?
- Because of what comes after it.

53
00:03:56,548 --> 00:03:58,390
<i>Past the glass, the field is gone.</i>

54
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:02,653
- The fog?
- It's up off the river already.

55
00:04:03,632 --> 00:04:08,441
- It keeps its own timetable, that fog.
- Nothing keeps a timetable tonight.

56
00:04:20,181 --> 00:04:21,936
<i>The telephone. Two rings.</i>

57
00:04:22,631 --> 00:04:23,631
Kilcarn.

58
00:04:24,231 --> 00:04:25,231
<i>Mam. It's me.</i>

59
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:27,280
Rosaleen.

60
00:04:27,694 --> 00:04:30,060
<i>I said I'd ring. You all right?</i>

61
00:04:30,515 --> 00:04:31,515
I'm working.

62
00:04:32,647 --> 00:04:33,928
<i>You know what I mean.</i>

63
00:04:34,630 --> 00:04:36,674
The 22:40 is twenty minutes down.

64
00:04:37,107 --> 00:04:38,107
<i>Mam.</i>

65
00:04:39,272 --> 00:04:40,880
The line doesn't care.

66
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:42,626
<i>That's not an answer.</i>

67
00:04:43,108 --> 00:04:44,377
It's the one I have.

68
00:04:45,508 --> 00:04:47,957
<i>Ring me when you're done.
Whatever hour it is.</i>

69
00:04:54,770 --> 00:04:57,247
- Your daughter?
- Put the kettle on again.

70
00:04:58,016 --> 00:05:01,355
- That one's gone cold.
- Then it's gone cold.

71
00:05:02,066 --> 00:05:04,156
{\an8}BRANCH LINE CLOSED FROM MONDAY

72
00:05:04,792 --> 00:05:06,938
<i>A notice, drawing-pinned to the wall.</i>

73
00:05:07,422 --> 00:05:11,621
- They put that up in August.
- Nobody ever took it down.

74
00:05:19,189 --> 00:05:20,793
Is the 22:40 lifting at all?

75
00:05:21,741 --> 00:05:23,386
Twenty-three down now.

76
00:05:24,284 --> 00:05:28,630
The fog came early. It came before
anyone had asked it to.

77
00:05:33,256 --> 00:05:38,219
- Can they not run her straight through?
- Not without the tablet.

78
00:05:39,009 --> 00:05:42,580
No tablet, no train.
That is the whole of the branch.

79
00:05:47,150 --> 00:05:51,840
- And if she's stuck at Ballyfin Halt?
- Then she's stuck at Ballyfin Halt.

80
00:05:52,311 --> 00:05:53,780
On the last night of all.

81
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:56,394
The line doesn't care.

82
00:06:02,876 --> 00:06:05,134
<i>The up home is a smear of red.</i>

83
00:06:05,999 --> 00:06:09,798
- Can you see the up home from here?
- I can see where it is.

84
00:06:10,702 --> 00:06:12,485
That is not the same thing.

85
00:06:13,056 --> 00:06:15,072
It is after thirty-eight years.

86
00:06:20,950 --> 00:06:22,853
<i>Kilcarn. Ballyfin calling.</i>

87
00:06:26,389 --> 00:06:27,389
Kilcarn.

88
00:06:28,122 --> 00:06:30,189
<i>She's away. Twenty-four late.</i>

89
00:06:31,306 --> 00:06:32,840
Is the fog signal manned?

90
00:06:33,307 --> 00:06:35,135
<i>He's out at the board this minute.</i>

91
00:06:46,821 --> 00:06:49,037
[the block bell, two beats and a pause]

92
00:06:49,711 --> 00:06:52,379
- Is that her?
- That's Section C asking.

93
00:06:56,059 --> 00:06:57,845
<i>Her hand goes to the commutator.</i>

94
00:06:58,281 --> 00:06:59,310
What do you do now?

95
00:07:00,431 --> 00:07:01,431
I accept her.

96
00:07:02,163 --> 00:07:04,573
<i>Two beats go back down the wire.</i>

97
00:07:05,051 --> 00:07:06,411
Line clear to Section C.

98
00:07:07,569 --> 00:07:09,565
<i>The needle swings over and holds.</i>

99
00:07:10,251 --> 00:07:13,423
- She's in the section?
- She's in Section C. Now we wait.

100
00:07:14,389 --> 00:07:16,093
<i>Fog moves on the glass, slow.</i>

101
00:07:27,939 --> 00:07:29,350
[rain easing on the glass]

102
00:07:29,822 --> 00:07:33,725
- There. It's giving up.
- It isn't giving up. It's changing.

103
00:07:34,185 --> 00:07:35,280
- Into what?
- Fog.

104
00:07:35,956 --> 00:07:38,354
It comes up the cutting off the river.

105
00:07:39,358 --> 00:07:43,407
- You can tell that from in here?
- I can tell it from the sound.

106
00:07:48,910 --> 00:07:51,072
Rain stops hitting. It starts sitting.

107
00:07:51,599 --> 00:07:53,562
<i>Below, the fields are going white.</i>

108
00:07:54,193 --> 00:07:55,274
That was quick.

109
00:07:55,773 --> 00:07:58,003
The fog keeps its own timetable.

110
00:07:58,847 --> 00:08:02,667
- It wasn't due till after two.
- Due. Listen to yourself.

111
00:08:03,701 --> 00:08:04,887
Put the kettle on.

112
00:08:05,630 --> 00:08:08,606
- Now?
- Now, while your hands are idle.

113
00:08:15,781 --> 00:08:18,377
- How many in the lever frame?
- Thirty-two.

114
00:08:19,449 --> 00:08:22,244
<i>Dessie walks the lever frame, touching each.</i>

115
00:08:22,663 --> 00:08:24,580
Twenty-six. Twenty-seven.

116
00:08:25,669 --> 00:08:28,015
Twenty-eight. Twenty-nine. Thirty.

117
00:08:28,988 --> 00:08:31,227
- What are you at?
- Making sure of it.

118
00:08:32,046 --> 00:08:33,046
Thirty-one.

119
00:08:44,457 --> 00:08:46,337
[the telephone bell, two rings]

120
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:47,960
Kilcarn Junction.

121
00:08:48,510 --> 00:08:49,510
<i>Mam. It's me.</i>

122
00:08:49,928 --> 00:08:50,928
Rosaleen.

123
00:08:51,755 --> 00:08:54,095
<i>You sound like you're down a well.</i>

124
00:08:54,909 --> 00:08:57,598
It's the fog. It takes the edge off.

125
00:08:58,379 --> 00:09:00,066
<i>Is the young fella still there?</i>

126
00:09:01,011 --> 00:09:03,115
He is. He's full of questions.

127
00:09:04,083 --> 00:09:05,649
<i>I did the back room out, Mam.</i>

128
00:09:06,114 --> 00:09:08,613
<i>Painted. There's a bed in it now.</i>

129
00:09:09,288 --> 00:09:10,288
Whose bed?

130
00:09:12,627 --> 00:09:14,014
<i>Yours. If you'd have it.</i>

131
00:09:14,470 --> 00:09:15,470
I have a bed.

132
00:09:16,452 --> 00:09:19,641
<i>You have a house with no reason left in it.</i>

133
00:09:20,588 --> 00:09:21,760
The house is fine.

134
00:09:25,762 --> 00:09:27,582
<i>I'm not asking about the house.</i>

135
00:09:28,089 --> 00:09:29,819
I know what you're asking.

136
00:09:30,696 --> 00:09:32,284
<i>The line shuts tomorrow, Mam.</i>

137
00:09:32,915 --> 00:09:34,482
I know what day it is.

138
00:09:35,646 --> 00:09:37,506
<i>So come down off it. Come to us.</i>

139
00:09:38,266 --> 00:09:39,885
The line doesn't care.

140
00:09:40,532 --> 00:09:42,549
<i>I'm not talking about the line.</i>

141
00:09:43,447 --> 00:09:44,447
Neither am I.

142
00:09:45,241 --> 00:09:46,463
I'll ring you Sunday.

143
00:09:53,818 --> 00:09:56,137
<i>She sets the receiver down square.</i>

144
00:09:56,930 --> 00:09:58,846
- Was that Rosaleen?
- It was.

145
00:09:59,653 --> 00:10:03,675
- She's worried about you.
- She's worried about the house.

146
00:10:04,328 --> 00:10:06,563
<i>The up home is gone from view.</i>

147
00:10:07,462 --> 00:10:11,020
- I can't see the up home.
- You won't see it again tonight.

148
00:10:12,034 --> 00:10:13,382
The fog picks its nights.

149
00:10:14,347 --> 00:10:16,495
- Does it?
- It picked this one.

150
00:10:17,486 --> 00:10:20,027
We work the block. Nothing changes.

151
00:10:26,771 --> 00:10:31,480
- Is the tablet back in the instrument?
- Since the 22:40 went down.

152
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:41,116
Nothing on the down line till the last.
Nothing on the up line at all.

153
00:10:46,069 --> 00:10:49,696
- Is there anyone at Ballyfin Halt?
- A lamp. Nobody to light it.

154
00:10:50,410 --> 00:10:52,547
You keep the block honest either way.

155
00:11:04,002 --> 00:11:06,560
<i>The telephone. Agnes lets it ring.</i>

156
00:11:07,697 --> 00:11:08,828
Kilcarn Junction.

157
00:11:09,392 --> 00:11:11,272
<i>Mrs. Toole. Bracewell, district.</i>

158
00:11:12,336 --> 00:11:13,878
<i>You have fog, I'm told.</i>

159
00:11:14,700 --> 00:11:15,939
We have. Thick with it.

160
00:11:16,578 --> 00:11:18,958
<i>Fog signal arrangements in hand?</i>

161
00:11:23,490 --> 00:11:25,487
Going down within the hour.

162
00:11:26,454 --> 00:11:28,064
<i>Good. Now. As to tomorrow.</i>

163
00:11:29,259 --> 00:11:30,259
Go on.

164
00:11:33,016 --> 00:11:34,793
<i>There will be a letter. In time.</i>

165
00:11:35,395 --> 00:11:36,395
Saying what?

166
00:11:37,524 --> 00:11:39,435
<i>It isn't drafted. Not by me.</i>

167
00:11:40,284 --> 00:11:42,117
Am I finished after tomorrow?

168
00:11:43,096 --> 00:11:44,627
<i>Nobody has used that word.</i>

169
00:11:48,751 --> 00:11:49,751
I'm using it.

170
00:11:50,386 --> 00:11:52,708
<i>Arrangements are being looked at.</i>

171
00:11:53,444 --> 00:11:54,658
Looked at by who?

172
00:11:55,536 --> 00:11:57,722
<i>I'll come back to you. Goodnight.</i>

173
00:12:06,015 --> 00:12:08,957
- What did he say?
- Nothing. At length.

174
00:12:09,815 --> 00:12:13,675
- He must have said something.
- He said it very carefully.

175
00:12:14,384 --> 00:12:17,701
- Are you kept on or not?
- He didn't say that either.

176
00:12:18,359 --> 00:12:20,970
That kettle boiled a half hour ago.

177
00:12:21,912 --> 00:12:25,777
- I'll wet the tea now.
- There's two cups there since ten.

178
00:12:31,416 --> 00:12:32,747
Nobody drank them either.

179
00:12:40,569 --> 00:12:42,025
Can I ask you a thing?

180
00:12:44,184 --> 00:12:45,679
You'll ask it anyway.

181
00:12:48,995 --> 00:12:51,363
Thirty-eight years in the one box.

182
00:12:52,247 --> 00:12:53,802
Did you ever want out of it?

183
00:12:54,852 --> 00:12:56,744
<i>Agnes goes on writing in the book.</i>

184
00:12:57,192 --> 00:13:00,569
The fog signal goes down now.
The tin case is under the stairs.

185
00:13:09,668 --> 00:13:11,420
[boots on the wooden stairs]

186
00:13:12,092 --> 00:13:13,952
<i>Dessie in the fog with the tin.</i>

187
00:13:14,462 --> 00:13:18,290
- How far down?
- Past the up home. You'll feel it.

188
00:13:18,838 --> 00:13:23,341
- I can't see a thing down here.
- You'll feel the ballast change.

189
00:13:23,829 --> 00:13:27,074
Clip the fog signal to the rail head, square.

190
00:13:27,576 --> 00:13:32,697
- And when a wheel goes over it?
- It bangs. That's the whole idea.

191
00:13:36,424 --> 00:13:40,555
Seems a lot of trouble for a line
that's finished in the morning.

192
00:13:41,608 --> 00:13:43,279
The line doesn't care.

193
00:13:43,901 --> 00:13:45,611
<i>He kneels down to the wet rail.</i>

194
00:13:46,255 --> 00:13:49,131
- Fog signal set!
- Come up. Mind the steps.

195
00:13:58,930 --> 00:14:00,758
Now put Section C back for me.

196
00:14:01,621 --> 00:14:03,054
{\an8}SECTION C - UP HOME

197
00:14:04,048 --> 00:14:05,936
<i>He takes the lever in both hands.</i>

198
00:14:06,946 --> 00:14:10,582
- It's not coming over.
- Pull it. Don't wrestle it.

199
00:14:11,228 --> 00:14:13,188
[the lever frame, a catch not taking]

200
00:14:15,868 --> 00:14:19,288
- Agnes. It's dead in my hand.
- Leave it. Don't touch anything.

201
00:14:29,537 --> 00:14:31,074
[rain stopping, all at once]

202
00:14:31,612 --> 00:14:33,077
- Is that not better?
- No.

203
00:14:34,094 --> 00:14:35,791
Rain, you can see through.

204
00:14:39,264 --> 00:14:41,657
- I can't see the down line.
- No.

205
00:14:44,785 --> 00:14:47,302
The fog came up the cutting before midnight.

206
00:14:48,364 --> 00:14:50,794
- Is that early?
- It keeps its own hours.

207
00:14:55,030 --> 00:14:57,058
- Twenty-nine.
- What about it?

208
00:15:01,349 --> 00:15:04,067
It went over and it won't come home.

209
00:15:05,240 --> 00:15:09,044
- Is it the frame?
- It's out on the ground. Section C.

210
00:15:09,618 --> 00:15:13,134
Detection won't prove.
So the up home stays on.

211
00:15:14,078 --> 00:15:17,291
- And the block?
- Blocked till someone goes down to it.

212
00:15:22,717 --> 00:15:24,037
Kilcarn Junction. Toole.

213
00:15:28,215 --> 00:15:30,220
<i>Mrs. Toole. It's gone three.</i>

214
00:15:30,867 --> 00:15:33,110
Twenty-nine has failed at Section C.

215
00:15:33,861 --> 00:15:35,209
<i>Failed in what sense?</i>

216
00:15:39,572 --> 00:15:43,965
I can't hand a driver the tablet
for a road I can't prove.

217
00:15:44,886 --> 00:15:46,114
Send me a fitter.

218
00:15:46,657 --> 00:15:50,964
<i>At this hour, on this branch,
there is no fitter to send.</i>

219
00:15:51,632 --> 00:15:54,605
<i>And I'd sooner nobody went out
on the ground in fog.</i>

220
00:15:58,594 --> 00:16:00,745
So it's my name on it, either way.

221
00:16:01,607 --> 00:16:03,671
<i>You have it in hand. Good night.</i>

222
00:16:14,907 --> 00:16:16,972
- What did he say?
- Nothing. Twice.

223
00:16:17,918 --> 00:16:20,390
- So nobody's coming.
- You're going down.

224
00:16:21,137 --> 00:16:24,015
- Me.
- I'm on the frame. It has to be you.

225
00:16:24,643 --> 00:16:26,494
Put the kettle on before you go.

226
00:16:27,024 --> 00:16:29,437
- Now?
- It'll be boiled when you're back.

227
00:16:32,908 --> 00:16:34,091
The bar. The lamp.

228
00:16:34,840 --> 00:16:39,963
- And the fog signal. Where does it go?
- On the rail, ten yards above you.

229
00:16:40,653 --> 00:16:43,058
If anything comes, you'll hear it go.

230
00:16:43,771 --> 00:16:45,249
You're counting again.

231
00:16:45,994 --> 00:16:47,861
Twenty-eight. Twenty-nine. Thirty.

232
00:16:48,481 --> 00:16:49,481
Thirty-one.

233
00:16:58,217 --> 00:16:59,743
[boots on wet ballast]

234
00:17:00,793 --> 00:17:03,108
{\an8}DANGER - POINTS WORKED FROM THE BOX

235
00:17:05,557 --> 00:17:07,170
Grand. That's grand, that is.

236
00:17:16,123 --> 00:17:17,351
Kilcarn. C post.

237
00:17:18,518 --> 00:17:19,518
<i>Go on.</i>

238
00:17:25,447 --> 00:17:27,030
I can't see the box from here.

239
00:17:30,987 --> 00:17:32,972
<i>You're not down there to see me.</i>

240
00:17:33,490 --> 00:17:34,754
<i>What's in front of you?</i>

241
00:17:35,541 --> 00:17:37,257
The blades are open. An inch.

242
00:17:38,156 --> 00:17:39,474
<i>Which one? Left or right?</i>

243
00:17:40,628 --> 00:17:42,915
Left. The one nearest my boot.

244
00:17:43,355 --> 00:17:45,562
<i>Look down into it. Don't touch.</i>

245
00:17:46,167 --> 00:17:48,770
There's a stone in there the size of my fist.

246
00:17:49,261 --> 00:17:51,004
<i>Use the bar. The long end.</i>

247
00:17:51,669 --> 00:17:53,162
Come on... Come on...

248
00:17:58,225 --> 00:17:59,891
It's out. It's out of there.

249
00:18:09,524 --> 00:18:11,034
Stand back from the blades.

250
00:18:11,823 --> 00:18:12,823
<i>Standing back.</i>

251
00:18:13,609 --> 00:18:14,674
Easing twenty-nine.

252
00:18:15,735 --> 00:18:17,123
<i>It's moving. It's moving-</i>

253
00:18:17,928 --> 00:18:18,928
<i>It's stopped.</i>

254
00:18:20,011 --> 00:18:21,584
Then it isn't the stone.

255
00:18:27,551 --> 00:18:28,855
<i>Look at the rodding.</i>

256
00:18:29,818 --> 00:18:32,367
The rod's off. It's down in the muck.

257
00:18:33,339 --> 00:18:34,458
<i>Is it off its roller?</i>

258
00:18:35,273 --> 00:18:36,273
Clean off it.

259
00:18:40,385 --> 00:18:42,341
<i>Then you're lifting it back on.</i>

260
00:18:43,223 --> 00:18:44,223
It's heavy.

261
00:18:44,672 --> 00:18:46,312
<i>It's iron. Legs, not your back.</i>

262
00:18:47,046 --> 00:18:49,885
The fog's sitting on the line like it owns it.

263
00:18:51,040 --> 00:18:52,040
<i>Tonight it does.</i>

264
00:19:01,803 --> 00:19:03,770
[the telephone, loud in the box]

265
00:19:04,621 --> 00:19:05,857
<i>Agnes? Are you there?</i>

266
00:19:07,011 --> 00:19:08,505
Don't move your hands.

267
00:19:09,560 --> 00:19:10,795
<i>Mam? I can't sleep.</i>

268
00:19:11,210 --> 00:19:13,409
Rosaleen. I have a lad on the ground.

269
00:19:14,164 --> 00:19:15,172
<i>Are you all right?</i>

270
00:19:15,715 --> 00:19:16,715
Not now.

271
00:19:17,612 --> 00:19:19,467
<i>Agnes. Agnes, my hands are in it.</i>

272
00:19:20,232 --> 00:19:22,125
I'm here. I never went anywhere.

273
00:19:30,094 --> 00:19:31,094
<i>Lift it now.</i>

274
00:19:31,971 --> 00:19:33,150
It's up. It's on.

275
00:19:34,126 --> 00:19:36,174
<i>Get your hands out of there.</i>

276
00:19:36,673 --> 00:19:37,673
Hands clear.

277
00:19:38,353 --> 00:19:39,353
<i>Say it again.</i>

278
00:19:41,759 --> 00:19:42,759
Hands clear!

279
00:19:43,606 --> 00:19:44,774
<i>Easing twenty-nine.</i>

280
00:19:45,853 --> 00:19:48,043
It's closing. It's home. It's home.

281
00:19:49,233 --> 00:19:50,355
<i>Detection's proved.</i>

282
00:19:51,059 --> 00:19:52,648
<i>Section C is mine again.</i>

283
00:20:02,711 --> 00:20:04,517
You never answered me. In the box.

284
00:20:05,466 --> 00:20:07,130
<i>I heard you the first time.</i>

285
00:20:08,226 --> 00:20:10,447
<i>Everything else wanted something.</i>

286
00:20:11,549 --> 00:20:12,863
<i>The line doesn't care.</i>

287
00:20:13,869 --> 00:20:14,983
That's not an answer.

288
00:20:15,698 --> 00:20:17,807
<i>It's the one I have. Come up.</i>

289
00:20:24,852 --> 00:20:26,550
- Sit down.
- I'm grand.

290
00:20:27,385 --> 00:20:28,713
The kettle's boiled dry.

291
00:20:29,602 --> 00:20:31,643
- Fill it. Two cups.
- Right.

292
00:20:36,862 --> 00:20:39,273
- I can't hold the cup.
- Put it down, then.

293
00:20:40,304 --> 00:20:42,545
- It'll go cold.
- It usually does.

294
00:20:43,138 --> 00:20:45,107
- If I'd been slower-
- You weren't.

295
00:20:46,158 --> 00:20:48,585
Thirty-eight years.
That's the worst of them.

296
00:20:49,454 --> 00:20:50,674
The line doesn't care.

297
00:20:51,473 --> 00:20:54,851
- You keep saying that.
- It keeps being true.

298
00:20:55,755 --> 00:20:57,758
[the block bell, two beats]

299
00:20:58,257 --> 00:21:00,956
- That's Ballyfin Halt, offering.
- The last one.

300
00:21:01,734 --> 00:21:03,460
- Do I take it?
- Take it.

301
00:21:09,477 --> 00:21:11,542
[the fog signal, far off, twice]

302
00:21:12,070 --> 00:21:14,677
- Was that her?
- That was the fog signal.

303
00:21:15,579 --> 00:21:20,052
- Something set it off.
- Water on the cap. Nothing set it off.

304
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:23,069
She hasn't left Ballyfin Halt yet.

305
00:21:25,256 --> 00:21:30,559
It came up the cutting after us.
Went round the back and tried the door.

306
00:21:33,757 --> 00:21:35,077
Fog doesn't try doors.

307
00:21:35,539 --> 00:21:37,732
It's been at that glass since two.

308
00:21:38,227 --> 00:21:39,693
The line doesn't care.

309
00:21:40,821 --> 00:21:44,128
- About the fog?
- About any of it. Sit down.

310
00:21:54,792 --> 00:21:59,744
- That's Ballyfin Halt on the bell.
- Three, pause, one. Answer it.

311
00:22:05,173 --> 00:22:07,338
- Me?
- You. It's your section.

312
00:22:07,771 --> 00:22:09,913
Repeat it back. Don't be polite to it.

313
00:22:11,065 --> 00:22:13,275
Now peg the block to line clear.

314
00:22:13,877 --> 00:22:16,580
- It's stiff.
- It's forty years stiff. Lean on it.

315
00:22:17,550 --> 00:22:19,342
Line clear for the last up train.

316
00:22:20,085 --> 00:22:23,036
- Is that the whole of it?
- That's the whole of it.

317
00:22:23,967 --> 00:22:26,532
Thirty-eight years of the whole of it.

318
00:22:35,641 --> 00:22:37,808
- You'll miss it.
- I'll miss the wages.

319
00:22:38,589 --> 00:22:40,684
Pull off the up home. Four, then two.

320
00:22:41,410 --> 00:22:44,176
- Four's off.
- Say it the way it's said.

321
00:22:44,738 --> 00:22:47,341
Up home off for the last up train.

322
00:22:48,311 --> 00:22:51,972
- Does that matter tonight?
- It matters tonight.

323
00:22:52,614 --> 00:22:54,954
I'd like the last one the same as the rest.

324
00:22:55,724 --> 00:22:59,417
- Fair enough.
- And don't lean on the lever frame.

325
00:23:05,230 --> 00:23:07,198
She's on the block at Section C.

326
00:23:07,913 --> 00:23:11,624
- I can't see her.
- You can hear her. That's plenty.

327
00:23:12,213 --> 00:23:14,268
[a train coming up, slow, in fog]

328
00:23:18,151 --> 00:23:20,197
She's feeling her way through it.

329
00:23:21,025 --> 00:23:22,668
The fog won't let her run.

330
00:23:23,170 --> 00:23:27,797
- Will Fintan even stop?
- He'll stop. He has the tablet on him.

331
00:23:28,372 --> 00:23:32,172
- And the down line?
- Dead since the 22:40 went down.

332
00:23:40,255 --> 00:23:41,688
<i>Agnes! Are you decent?</i>

333
00:23:42,490 --> 00:23:43,950
The door was never locked.

334
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:47,774
- God, the smell of this box.
- Paraffin and mice. Same as ever.

335
00:23:48,287 --> 00:23:51,152
- Twenty years I'm up these steps.
- Twenty-two.

336
00:23:51,942 --> 00:23:56,289
- That's the tablet back.
- Put it in the machine yourself.

337
00:23:57,182 --> 00:24:01,049
- It's your machine.
- It's nobody's machine after six.

338
00:24:01,803 --> 00:24:04,214
I've had it in my pocket since half ten.

339
00:24:04,957 --> 00:24:06,510
The 22:40 went down with it.

340
00:24:09,508 --> 00:24:12,041
Now it comes back up the up line.

341
00:24:16,626 --> 00:24:18,627
{\an8}KILCARN JUNCTION - BALLYFIN HALT

342
00:24:19,603 --> 00:24:25,303
- Fourteen on her tonight.
- Fourteen. Two of them came for the last of it.

343
00:24:26,295 --> 00:24:29,376
- Did anyone tell you what happens now?
- To the box?

344
00:24:30,252 --> 00:24:31,252
To you.

345
00:24:31,827 --> 00:24:33,422
- No.
- No. They wouldn't.

346
00:24:34,365 --> 00:24:36,349
You told me this on my first trip.

347
00:24:37,158 --> 00:24:38,329
The line doesn't care.

348
00:24:38,785 --> 00:24:42,891
- I never said that to a boy of nineteen.
- You did. Those words.

349
00:24:49,442 --> 00:24:53,565
- Good luck, Agnes.
- Mind the steps, Fintan. They're greasy.

350
00:24:56,222 --> 00:24:58,152
<i>I'll see you in the town, so.</i>

351
00:24:59,224 --> 00:25:00,224
No, you won't.

352
00:25:01,396 --> 00:25:02,396
<i>No. I won't.</i>

353
00:25:03,260 --> 00:25:07,024
- I'll put the kettle on.
- You've had it on three times.

354
00:25:07,924 --> 00:25:11,539
- And nobody's drunk any of it.
- Put it on again.

355
00:25:21,914 --> 00:25:26,481
- That's not the block bell.
- It's the telephone. You take it.

356
00:25:27,388 --> 00:25:28,388
Kilcarn Junction.

357
00:25:33,051 --> 00:25:34,810
<i>Is that Dessie? Put her on.</i>

358
00:25:35,513 --> 00:25:38,661
- It's Rosaleen.
- I know the ring of it.

359
00:25:39,129 --> 00:25:40,129
<i>Mother.</i>

360
00:25:40,917 --> 00:25:41,917
I'm here.

361
00:25:42,895 --> 00:25:44,700
<i>You said you'd ring me at four.</i>

362
00:25:45,829 --> 00:25:48,130
The train's gone up. That's the end.

363
00:25:48,655 --> 00:25:50,284
<i>Will I collect you at six?</i>

364
00:25:50,783 --> 00:25:52,418
There's a bus at twenty past.

365
00:25:52,858 --> 00:25:54,711
<i>I'm not asking about buses.</i>

366
00:26:00,357 --> 00:26:01,564
Come at six, so.

367
00:26:02,498 --> 00:26:03,498
Rosaleen.

368
00:26:04,343 --> 00:26:05,343
<i>What?</i>

369
00:26:06,120 --> 00:26:07,571
I did want you to stay.

370
00:26:08,709 --> 00:26:11,773
I never said it out loud. That was the wrong of it.

371
00:26:12,459 --> 00:26:14,925
There's tea made here. Go to bed.

372
00:26:16,106 --> 00:26:17,471
<i>You never drink it.</i>

373
00:26:21,528 --> 00:26:22,528
No.

374
00:26:31,562 --> 00:26:32,795
<i>Mrs. Toole. Bracewell.</i>

375
00:26:33,521 --> 00:26:35,384
<i>I said I would ring before six.</i>

376
00:26:35,837 --> 00:26:37,617
You said you'd ring before two.

377
00:26:43,588 --> 00:26:44,588
<i>Yes.</i>

378
00:26:45,524 --> 00:26:47,726
<i>The box is not to be retained.</i>

379
00:26:48,489 --> 00:26:50,191
<i>It is scheduled for removal.</i>

380
00:26:50,927 --> 00:26:52,045
Say the other word.

381
00:26:53,056 --> 00:26:54,654
<i>Demolition. In the spring.</i>

382
00:26:55,282 --> 00:26:57,501
<i>There is no further post for you.</i>

383
00:26:59,781 --> 00:27:01,774
<i>There was never going to be one.</i>

384
00:27:02,811 --> 00:27:04,536
<i>I should have said that at two.</i>

385
00:27:05,156 --> 00:27:07,057
- Is he gone?
- He said "removal".

386
00:27:15,405 --> 00:27:16,910
- It's going.
- What is?

387
00:27:17,956 --> 00:27:20,580
Look at that. Off the glass and all.

388
00:27:21,435 --> 00:27:25,152
- It came for the last train.
- It got it. It's away off now.

389
00:27:25,946 --> 00:27:27,869
Twenty-nine. Thirty. Thirty-one.

390
00:27:31,103 --> 00:27:34,401
- Thirty-one. Same as always.
- Count the white one.

391
00:27:35,019 --> 00:27:38,104
- The white one's out of use.
- It's a lever. Count it.

392
00:27:42,044 --> 00:27:43,044
Thirty-two.

393
00:27:52,013 --> 00:27:55,120
- Stand back from the frame.
- It's yours anyway.

394
00:27:55,836 --> 00:27:58,682
That's Kilcarn Junction out of the block.

395
00:27:59,714 --> 00:28:01,390
[the box very quiet now]

396
00:28:02,038 --> 00:28:03,486
[the kettle, at last]

397
00:28:04,174 --> 00:28:08,541
- That's yours. Mind, it's stewed.
- Thirty-eight years for a cup of tea.

398
00:28:09,661 --> 00:28:12,293
- You're drinking it.
- I am. It's desperate.

399
00:28:13,198 --> 00:28:14,441
The line doesn't care.

400
00:28:15,607 --> 00:28:17,265
{\an8}KILCARN JUNCTION CLOSED 1987

